# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mathematical formalism for the latest version of Qiskit noise learning"
msgstr "Formalismo matemático de la última versión de Qiskit noise learning"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mathematical formalism"
msgstr "Formalismo matemático"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title #
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mathematical formalism {#mathematical-formalism}"
msgstr "Formalismo matemático {#mathematical-formalism}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Given the task of learning Pauli noise for a set of Clifford gates, a common analysis technique is to track how individual Pauli operators are transformed through a sequence of gate applications (under the assumption that the gates are Clifford and the noise is a Pauli channel, a single Pauli will always be mapped to another Pauli up to a scalar that is a function of the noise). Obviously, the evolution of *any* state under such a sequence can be captured by a linear combination of such trajectories, but under the assumption that we always prepare the state in a Pauli eigenstate, and always measure and post-process the results to compute the expectation value of a Pauli operator, the relationship between the expectation value and the noise model parameters will *always* depend on only a single such trajectory. This follows from the simple fact that the initial state is a linear combination of Pauli operators, each gate and noise model maps Paulis to Paulis and preserves their orthogonality, and the final expectation value “selects” only one of the Paulis in the final decomposition before measurement.\n"
msgstr "Dada la tarea de aprender el ruido de Pauli de un conjunto de puertas Clifford, una técnica de análisis común consiste en seguir cómo se transforman los operadores de Pauli individuales a través de una secuencia de aplicaciones de puertas (bajo el supuesto de que las puertas son Clifford y el ruido es un canal de Pauli, un único Pauli siempre se transformará en otro Pauli salvo por un escalar que es función del ruido). Obviamente, la evolución de *cualquier* estado bajo tal secuencia puede capturarse mediante una combinación lineal de dichas trayectorias, pero bajo el supuesto de que siempre preparamos el estado en un autoestado de Pauli, y siempre medimos y posprocesamos los resultados para calcular el valor esperado de un operador de Pauli, la relación entre el valor esperado y los parámetros del modelo de ruido *siempre* dependerá de una única trayectoria de este tipo. Esto se deriva del simple hecho de que el estado inicial es una combinación lineal de operadores de Pauli, cada puerta y modelo de ruido transforma Paulis en Paulis y preserva su ortogonalidad, y el valor esperado final “selecciona” solo uno de los Paulis de la descomposición final antes de la medición.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This type of reasoning appears in many parallel research tracks in noise learning, including in the Pauli gate-set learning literature \\[[1](#id17 \"Senrui Chen, Zhihan Zhang, Liang Jiang, and Steven T. Flammia. Efficient Self-Consistent Learning of Gate Set Pauli Noise. PRX Quantum, 7(1):010305, 2026. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/1pnv-t9px, doi:10.1103/1pnv-t9px.\"), [2](#id20 \"Edward H. Chen, Senrui Chen, Laurin E. Fischer, Andrew Eddins, Luke C. G. Govia, Brad Mitchell, Andre He, Youngseok Kim, Liang Jiang, and Alireza Seif. Disambiguating pauli noise in quantum computers. PRX Quantum, pages, 2026. doi:10.1103/69wc-gzl6.\")], the ACES literature \\[[3](#id15 \"Steven T. Flammia. Averaged Circuit Eigenvalue Sampling. In 17th Conference on the Theory of Quantum Computation, Communication and Cryptography (TQC 2022), volume 232 of Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs), 4:1–4:10. Dagstuhl, Germany, 2022. Schloss Dagstuhl – Leibniz-Zentrum für Informatik. doi:10.4230/LIPIcs.TQC.2022.4.\")], and the cycle benchmarking literature \\[[4](#id14 \"Alexander Erhard, Joel J. Wallman, Lukas Postler, Michael Meth, Roman Stricker, Esteban A. Martinez, Philipp Schindler, Thomas Monz, Joseph Emerson, and Rainer Blatt. Characterizing large-scale quantum computers via cycle benchmarking. Nature Communications, 10(1):5347, 2019. doi:10.1038/s41467-019-13068-7.\")]. This reasoning was further generalized in \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")] to include Clifford-MCM gates (a Clifford gate followed by a projective mid-circuit measurement). This package most closely follows \\[[1](#id17 \"Senrui Chen, Zhihan Zhang, Liang Jiang, and Steven T. Flammia. Efficient Self-Consistent Learning of Gate Set Pauli Noise. PRX Quantum, 7(1):010305, 2026. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/1pnv-t9px, doi:10.1103/1pnv-t9px.\"), [5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")]. While not explicitly named, we adopt the *Pattern Transfer Graph* (PTG) formalism for describing how Pauli operators evolve through learning circuits, providing a direct data representation of *paths* through the graph.\n"
msgstr "Este tipo de razonamiento aparece en muchas líneas de investigación paralelas sobre aprendizaje de ruido, incluida la literatura de aprendizaje de conjuntos de puertas de Pauli \\[[1](#id17 \"Senrui Chen, Zhihan Zhang, Liang Jiang, and Steven T. Flammia. Efficient Self-Consistent Learning of Gate Set Pauli Noise. PRX Quantum, 7(1):010305, 2026. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/1pnv-t9px, doi:10.1103/1pnv-t9px.\"), [2](#id20 \"Edward H. Chen, Senrui Chen, Laurin E. Fischer, Andrew Eddins, Luke C. G. Govia, Brad Mitchell, Andre He, Youngseok Kim, Liang Jiang, and Alireza Seif. Disambiguating pauli noise in quantum computers. PRX Quantum, pages, 2026. doi:10.1103/69wc-gzl6.\")], la literatura de ACES \\[[3](#id15 \"Steven T. Flammia. Averaged Circuit Eigenvalue Sampling. In 17th Conference on the Theory of Quantum Computation, Communication and Cryptography (TQC 2022), volume 232 of Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs), 4:1–4:10. Dagstuhl, Germany, 2022. Schloss Dagstuhl – Leibniz-Zentrum für Informatik. doi:10.4230/LIPIcs.TQC.2022.4.\")] y la literatura de cycle benchmarking \\[[4](#id14 \"Alexander Erhard, Joel J. Wallman, Lukas Postler, Michael Meth, Roman Stricker, Esteban A. Martinez, Philipp Schindler, Thomas Monz, Joseph Emerson, and Rainer Blatt. Characterizing large-scale quantum computers via cycle benchmarking. Nature Communications, 10(1):5347, 2019. doi:10.1038/s41467-019-13068-7.\")]. Este razonamiento se generalizó aún más en \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")] para incluir puertas Clifford-MCM (una puerta Clifford seguida de una medición proyectiva a mitad de circuito). Este paquete sigue de cerca \\[[1](#id17 \"Senrui Chen, Zhihan Zhang, Liang Jiang, and Steven T. Flammia. Efficient Self-Consistent Learning of Gate Set Pauli Noise. PRX Quantum, 7(1):010305, 2026. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/1pnv-t9px, doi:10.1103/1pnv-t9px.\"), [5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")]. Aunque no se nombra explícitamente, adoptamos el formalismo del *Pattern Transfer Graph* (PTG) para describir cómo evolucionan los operadores de Pauli a través de los circuitos de aprendizaje, lo que proporciona una representación de datos directa de los *caminos* a través del grafo.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The following is a review of some core mathematical concepts from the literature. It is primarily meant to consolidate notation, and to serve as a conceptual documentation reference for the rest of the package.\n"
msgstr "A continuación se presenta un repaso de algunos conceptos matemáticos centrales de la literatura. Su objetivo principal es consolidar la notación y servir como referencia conceptual de documentación para el resto del paquete.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"background\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "1. Background {#1-background}"
msgstr "1. Antecedentes {#1-background}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"notation\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.1 Notation {#11-notation}"
msgstr "1.1 Notación {#11-notation}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For $K \\in \\mathbb{N}$, let $[K] = \\{0, 1, \\dots, K - 1\\}$. For a finite set $S \\subset \\mathbb{N}$ of qubit indices, let $\\mathcal{P}^S$ denote the set of *unphased* Pauli operators acting on those qubits. Note that we think of elements of $\\mathcal{P}^S$ as functions mapping $S \\rightarrow \\{I, X, Y, Z\\}$, so that for any $P \\in \\mathcal{P}^S$ and $T \\subseteq S$, $P|_T$ denotes the restriction of $P$ to the qubit subset $T$. Along these lines, for disjoint sets $S, T$, and $P \\in \\mathcal{P}^S$ and $Q \\in \\mathcal{P}^T$, $P \\otimes Q$ denotes the element of $\\mathcal{P}^{S \\cup T}$ such that $P \\otimes Q |_S = P$ and $P \\otimes Q |_T = Q$. This notation is helpful to avoid explicitly dealing with subsystem orderings, and to make it easy to describe restrictions.\n"
msgstr "Para $K \\in \\mathbb{N}$, sea $[K] = \\{0, 1, \\dots, K - 1\\}$. Para un conjunto finito $S \\subset \\mathbb{N}$ de índices de qubits, sea $\\mathcal{P}^S$ el conjunto de operadores de Pauli *sin fase* que actúan sobre esos qubits. Ten en cuenta que pensamos en los elementos de $\\mathcal{P}^S$ como funciones que asignan $S \\rightarrow \\{I, X, Y, Z\\}$, de modo que para cualquier $P \\in \\mathcal{P}^S$ y $T \\subseteq S$, $P|_T$ denota la restricción de $P$ al subconjunto de qubits $T$. En esta línea, para conjuntos disjuntos $S, T$, y $P \\in \\mathcal{P}^S$ y $Q \\in \\mathcal{P}^T$, $P \\otimes Q$ denota el elemento de $\\mathcal{P}^{S \\cup T}$ tal que $P \\otimes Q |_S = P$ y $P \\otimes Q |_T = Q$. Esta notación ayuda a evitar tratar explícitamente los órdenes de los subsistemas y facilita describir restricciones.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For finite subsets $M \\subset \\mathbb{N}$, we denote $\\mathbb{Z}_2^M$ as the set of the bit strings whose elements are indexed by $M$. Similarly to the above, we think of elements as functions mapping $M \\rightarrow \\mathbb{Z}_2$, so that we may easily describe substrings in terms of restrictions of the index set.\n"
msgstr "Para subconjuntos finitos $M \\subset \\mathbb{N}$, denotamos $\\mathbb{Z}_2^M$ como el conjunto de cadenas de bits cuyos elementos están indexados por $M$. De manera similar a lo anterior, pensamos en los elementos como funciones que asignan $M \\rightarrow \\mathbb{Z}_2$, de modo que podamos describir fácilmente subcadenas en términos de restricciones del conjunto de índices.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lastly, for a matrix $X$, we use $| X \\rangle\\!\\rangle$ to denote its vectorization. Based on the limited way in which we use this notation, it is not actually necessary to choose a specific vectorization convention. For a classical bit string $m$, we use the shorthand $| m \\rangle\\!\\rangle = | | m \\rangle\\langle m | \\rangle\\!\\rangle$.\n"
msgstr "Por último, para una matriz $X$, usamos $| X \\rangle\\!\\rangle$ para denotar su vectorización. Dada la forma limitada en que usamos esta notación, en realidad no es necesario elegir una convención de vectorización específica. Para una cadena de bits clásica $m$, usamos la abreviatura $| m \\rangle\\!\\rangle = | | m \\rangle\\langle m | \\rangle\\!\\rangle$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"quantum-instruments\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.2 Quantum instruments {#12-quantum-instruments}"
msgstr "1.2 Instrumentos cuánticos {#12-quantum-instruments}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A quantum operation producing classical bits $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M$ (for example, the result of measurement) is generally modeled as a linear map of the form:\n"
msgstr "Una operación cuántica que produce bits clásicos $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M$ (por ejemplo, el resultado de una medición) se modela generalmente como una aplicación lineal de la forma:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
" \\rho \\mapsto \\sum_{m \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} \\mathcal{E}_m(\\rho) \\otimes | m \\rangle\\langle m |, \n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
" \\rho \\mapsto \\sum_{m \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} \\mathcal{E}_m(\\rho) \\otimes | m \\rangle\\langle m |, \n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "where the set $\\{\\mathcal{E}_m: m \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M\\}$ are completely positive, and $\\sum_m \\mathcal{E}_m$ is trace-preserving. A set of completely positive maps $\\{\\mathcal{E}_m\\}$ satisfying these properties is called a *quantum instrument*. A unitary gate is a special case of an instrument with only one element that is a unitary operation.\n"
msgstr "donde el conjunto $\\{\\mathcal{E}_m: m \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M\\}$ es completamente positivo, y $\\sum_m \\mathcal{E}_m$ preserva la traza. Un conjunto de aplicaciones completamente positivas $\\{\\mathcal{E}_m\\}$ que satisface estas propiedades se denomina *instrumento cuántico*. Una puerta unitaria es un caso especial de instrumento con un solo elemento que es una operación unitaria.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"noisy-clifford-mcm-reset-gates\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "2. Noisy Clifford-MCM-reset gates {#2-noisy-clifford-mcm-reset-gates}"
msgstr "2. Puertas Clifford-MCM-reset con ruido {#2-noisy-clifford-mcm-reset-gates}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The formalism utilized in this package assumes every gate in the gate set to be characterized consists of the following sequence of operations on $K$ qubits:\n"
msgstr "El formalismo utilizado en este paquete supone que cada puerta del conjunto de puertas a caracterizar consiste en la siguiente secuencia de operaciones sobre $K$ qubits:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '1.  '
#, no-wrap
msgid "A Clifford operation on all qubits.\n"
msgstr "Una operación Clifford sobre todos los qubits.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '2.  '
#, no-wrap
msgid "A mid-circuit projective measurement along $Z$ on some subset of qubits $M \\subseteq [K]$.\n"
msgstr "Una medición proyectiva a mitad de circuito a lo largo de $Z$ sobre algún subconjunto de qubits $M \\subseteq [K]$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '3.  '
#, no-wrap
msgid "A mid-circuit reset to the $Z$ ground state on some subset of qubits $R \\subseteq [K]$.\n"
msgstr "Un reinicio a mitad de circuito al estado fundamental de $Z$ sobre algún subconjunto de qubits $R \\subseteq [K]$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is a general class of operations that includes unitary Clifford gates, measurement, state preparation, and any combination of the above. Note that we assume measurement and reset are always along the $Z$-axis for each qubit. While this is not strictly required, it is a common feature of many quantum computing modalities, and enables simplified representations and analysis.\n"
msgstr "Esta es una clase general de operaciones que incluye puertas Clifford unitarias, mediciones, preparación de estados y cualquier combinación de las anteriores. Ten en cuenta que suponemos que la medición y el reinicio siempre se realizan a lo largo del eje $Z$ para cada qubit. Aunque esto no es estrictamente necesario, es una característica común de muchas modalidades de computación cuántica y permite representaciones y análisis más simples.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As outlined in \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\"), [6](#id18 \"Stefanie J. Beale and Joel J. Wallman. Randomized compiling for subsystem measurements. 2023. URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2304.06599, doi:10.48550/arXiv.2304.06599.\")] for the no-reset case, if a specific twirling strategy is applied to a noisy instance of such a gate, then the action of the resulting operation can be modeled mathematically as a *uniform Pauli instrument*. That is, within the quantum instrument notation, $\\mathcal{E}_m = \\mathcal{U}_mG$, where $G$ is the Clifford unitary, and:\n"
msgstr "Como se describe en \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\"), [6](#id18 \"Stefanie J. Beale and Joel J. Wallman. Randomized compiling for subsystem measurements. 2023. URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2304.06599, doi:10.48550/arXiv.2304.06599.\")] para el caso sin reinicio, si se aplica una estrategia de twirling específica a una instancia ruidosa de una puerta de este tipo, entonces la acción de la operación resultante puede modelarse matemáticamente como un *instrumento de Pauli uniforme*. Es decir, dentro de la notación de instrumentos cuánticos, $\\mathcal{E}_m = \\mathcal{U}_mG$, donde $G$ es la unitaria Clifford, y:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
" \\mathcal{U}_m = \\sum_{a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} \\Lambda_{a,b} \\otimes | m + a \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\langle\\!\\langle m + b |, \n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
" \\mathcal{U}_m = \\sum_{a,b \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} \\Lambda_{a,b} \\otimes | m + a \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\langle\\!\\langle m + b |, \n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "where each $\\Lambda_{a,b}$ is a sub-normalized Pauli channel on the unmeasured qubits $N = [K] \\setminus M$. It is implied by this being a quantum instrument that $\\sum_{a,b} \\Lambda_{a,b}$ is also trace-preserving. Some notes:\n"
msgstr "donde cada $\\Lambda_{a,b}$ es un canal de Pauli subnormalizado sobre los qubits no medidos $N = [K] \\setminus M$. Al tratarse de un instrumento cuántico, se sobreentiende que $\\sum_{a,b} \\Lambda_{a,b}$ también preserva la traza. Algunas notas:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The initial untwirled noise is modeled to include both “quantum” and “classical” errors: erroneous operations on the quantum registers, as well as mistakes in the measurement value reporting.\n"
msgstr "Se modela que el ruido inicial sin twirling incluye errores tanto “cuánticos” como “clásicos”: operaciones erróneas sobre los registros cuánticos, así como errores en el reporte del valor de la medición.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The noise map $\\Lambda_{a,b}$ is *independent* of the measurement outcome $m$.\n"
msgstr "El mapa de ruido $\\Lambda_{a,b}$ es *independiente* del resultado de la medición $m$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In words, a single term in the above sum represents observing a measurement outcome of $m$ when the measured state was $| m + b \\rangle$ (a misclassification if $b \\neq 0$), and when the output state on the measurement register is $| m + a \\rangle$ (the “wrong” state when $a \\neq 0$). The map $\\Lambda_{a,b}$ simultaneously encodes the action on the unmeasured qubits (conditioned on the measurement behavior) and the *probability* of the specific measurement behavior (through the normalization).\n"
msgstr "En palabras, un solo término de la suma anterior representa observar un resultado de medición $m$ cuando el estado medido era $| m + b \\rangle$ (una clasificación errónea si $b \\neq 0$), y cuando el estado de salida en el registro de medición es $| m + a \\rangle$ (el estado “incorrecto” cuando $a \\neq 0$). El mapa $\\Lambda_{a,b}$ codifica simultáneamente la acción sobre los qubits no medidos (condicionada al comportamiento de la medición) y la *probabilidad* del comportamiento de medición específico (a través de la normalización).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that we are not concerned here with the specifics of the twirling strategy: that such a strategy exists to put the channel into the above form is enough. Note that “finer” twirling strategies exist which can further restrict the form of the Pauli channels \\[[7](#id19 \"Ewout van den Berg and Pawel Wocjan. Techniques for learning sparse Pauli-Lindblad noise models. Quantum, 8:1556, 2024. URL: https://quantum-journal.org/papers/q-2024-12-10-1556/, doi:10.22331/q-2024-12-10-1556.\")]; however, we take the above form as the most general mathematical representation under consideration.\n"
msgstr "Ten en cuenta que aquí no nos interesan los detalles de la estrategia de twirling: basta con que exista una estrategia que ponga el canal en la forma anterior. Existen estrategias de twirling “más finas” que pueden restringir aún más la forma de los canales de Pauli \\[[7](#id19 \"Ewout van den Berg and Pawel Wocjan. Techniques for learning sparse Pauli-Lindblad noise models. Quantum, 8:1556, 2024. URL: https://quantum-journal.org/papers/q-2024-12-10-1556/, doi:10.22331/q-2024-12-10-1556.\")]; sin embargo, tomamos la forma anterior como la representación matemática más general considerada.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In Lemma 1 of \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")], it is shown that $\\mathcal{E}_m = \\mathcal{U}_mG$ can be rewritten as:\n"
msgstr "En el Lema 1 de \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")], se demuestra que $\\mathcal{E}_m = \\mathcal{U}_mG$ puede reescribirse como:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"equation-clifford-mcm-form\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
" \\mathcal{E}_m = \\frac{1}{2^{2|M| + |N|}}\\sum_{x, y \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M, Q \\in \\mathcal{P}^N} (-1)^{m \\cdot (x + y)}\n"
"\\lambda^Q_{x, y}| Q \\otimes Z^y \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(Q \\otimes Z^x) |. \n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
" \\mathcal{E}_m = \\frac{1}{2^{2|M| + |N|}}\\sum_{x, y \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M, Q \\in \\mathcal{P}^N} (-1)^{m \\cdot (x + y)}\n"
"\\lambda^Q_{x, y}| Q \\otimes Z^y \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(Q \\otimes Z^x) |. \n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "for some real numbers $\\lambda_{x,y}^Q$, which are called the *fidelities* of the instrument.\n"
msgstr "para ciertos números reales $\\lambda_{x,y}^Q$, que se denominan las *fidelidades* del instrumento.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Adding reset to this picture is relatively straightforward. A noisy reset operation on qubits $R \\subset [K]$ can be modeled according to the decomposition:\n"
msgstr "Agregar el reinicio a este panorama es relativamente sencillo. Una operación de reinicio ruidosa sobre los qubits $R \\subset [K]$ puede modelarse según la descomposición:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"equation-reset-form\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
" \\frac{1}{2^{|R|}}\\sum_{r \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{R}} \\lambda_r | Z^r \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\langle\\!\\langle I_R |. \n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
" \\frac{1}{2^{|R|}}\\sum_{r \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{R}} \\lambda_r | Z^r \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\langle\\!\\langle I_R |. \n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The above form explicitly utilizes the assumption that the reset is along the $Z$-axis for each qubit. Noise in the operation is encoded in the reset fidelities $\\lambda_r$, which are simply indexed by qubit subsets.\n"
msgstr "La forma anterior utiliza explícitamente el supuesto de que el reinicio se realiza a lo largo del eje $Z$ para cada qubit. El ruido de la operación se codifica en las fidelidades de reinicio $\\lambda_r$, que simplemente están indexadas por subconjuntos de qubits.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "An analog to Equation [(1)](#equation-clifford-mcm-form) that includes a reset operation at the end can be attained by simply composing it with Equation [(2)](#equation-reset-form). This composition, after some simplification, yields:\n"
msgstr "Un análogo de la Ecuación [(1)](#equation-clifford-mcm-form) que incluya una operación de reinicio al final se obtiene simplemente componiéndola con la Ecuación [(2)](#equation-reset-form). Esta composición, tras algunas simplificaciones, produce:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"equation-clifford-mcm-reset-form\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{split} \\frac{1}{2^{2|M|+|N|}} \\sum_{\\substack{x \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M, y \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{M \\cup R} \\\\ Q \\in\n"
"\\mathcal{P}^{N\\setminus R}}} (-1)^{m \\cdot (x + y|_M)} \\lambda^Q_{x,y}  | Q \\otimes\n"
"Z^y \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(Q \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^x) |. \\end{split}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{split} \\frac{1}{2^{2|M|+|N|}} \\sum_{\\substack{x \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M, y \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{M \\cup R} \\\\ Q \\in\n"
"\\mathcal{P}^{N\\setminus R}}} (-1)^{m \\cdot (x + y|_M)} \\lambda^Q_{x,y}  | Q \\otimes\n"
"Z^y \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(Q \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^x) |. \\end{split}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This decomposition indicates that the number of fidelities $\\lambda^Q_{x, y}$ is\n"
msgstr "Esta descomposición indica que el número de fidelidades $\\lambda^Q_{x, y}$ es\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"equation-num-fidelities\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"4^{|N \\setminus R|} 2^{|M|}2^{|M \\cup R|} = 4^{K}2^{|M|}2^{-|M \\cup R|} = 4^K 2^{-|R \\setminus M|}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"4^{|N \\setminus R|} 2^{|M|}2^{|M \\cup R|} = 4^{K}2^{|M|}2^{-|M \\cup R|} = 4^K 2^{-|R \\setminus M|}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Consider limiting cases: (1) For no measurement or reset, $M = R = \\emptyset$, and the expression yields $4^K$, the number of phase-less Pauli operators on $K$ qubits. (2) For $M = \\emptyset$ and $R=[K]$, this yields $2^K$, which is the number of subsets of $[K]$. Lastly, (3) for $M = [K]$ and $R = \\emptyset$, this yields $4^K$. Note that this may initially seem surprising: for standard learning, measurement and reset are typically considered to have the same number of fidelities: $2^K$. However, here the all-measurement gate in this context is understood not as a *terminal* measurement, but as an MCM, and as such fidelities corresponding to non-identity *outputs* of the measurement are also included (such as $y \\neq 0$ in Equation [(3)](#equation-clifford-mcm-reset-form)).\n"
msgstr "Consideremos casos límite: (1) Sin medición ni reinicio, $M = R = \\emptyset$, y la expresión da $4^K$, el número de operadores de Pauli sin fase sobre $K$ qubits. (2) Para $M = \\emptyset$ y $R=[K]$, resulta $2^K$, que es el número de subconjuntos de $[K]$. Por último, (3) para $M = [K]$ y $R = \\emptyset$, resulta $4^K$. Ten en cuenta que esto puede parecer sorprendente al principio: en el aprendizaje estándar, se suele considerar que la medición y el reinicio tienen el mismo número de fidelidades: $2^K$. Sin embargo, aquí la puerta de medición total en este contexto se entiende no como una medición *terminal*, sino como una MCM, y por ello también se incluyen las fidelidades correspondientes a *salidas* de la medición distintas de la identidad (como $y \\neq 0$ en la Ecuación [(3)](#equation-clifford-mcm-reset-form)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, it is important to note that any twirling strategy yielding the form in Equation [(1)](#equation-clifford-mcm-form) in the non-reset case still applies equally well to the non-trivial reset case with a simple modification. The potential problem is that such a twirling strategy may require operations on measured qubits *after* the measurement. Therefore, if a qubit is measured *and* reset, implementing the strategy seemingly requires inserting operations *within* the gate between the measurement and reset. However, given the nature of reset, any such operations have no effect, and can simply be skipped while still yielding the same desired structure.\n"
msgstr "Finalmente, es importante señalar que cualquier estrategia de twirling que produzca la forma de la Ecuación [(1)](#equation-clifford-mcm-form) en el caso sin reinicio se aplica igualmente bien al caso de reinicio no trivial con una modificación sencilla. El problema potencial es que dicha estrategia puede requerir operaciones sobre qubits medidos *después* de la medición. Por lo tanto, si un qubit se mide *y* se reinicia, implementar la estrategia parece requerir insertar operaciones *dentro* de la puerta entre la medición y el reinicio. Sin embargo, dada la naturaleza del reinicio, tales operaciones no tienen efecto y pueden omitirse sin más, obteniendo la misma estructura deseada.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"path-formalism\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "3. Path formalism {#3-path-formalism}"
msgstr "3. Formalismo de caminos {#3-path-formalism}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As described in the introduction, all learning algorithms for Pauli-type noise on Clifford gates are based around tracking how individual Paulis evolve through a circuit, picking up noise fidelity factors along the way. The expectation values of a circuit composed of such gates are therefore related simply to products of the underlying fidelities of the individual gates, and in this way model parameters can be inferred by *inverting* whatever sparse parameter-to-fidelity mapping is being assumed.\n"
msgstr "Como se describió en la introducción, todos los algoritmos de aprendizaje de ruido de tipo Pauli sobre puertas Clifford se basan en seguir cómo evolucionan los Paulis individuales a través de un circuito, acumulando factores de fidelidad de ruido por el camino. Por lo tanto, los valores esperados de un circuito compuesto por tales puertas se relacionan simplemente con productos de las fidelidades subyacentes de las puertas individuales, y de este modo los parámetros del modelo pueden inferirse *invirtiendo* la correspondencia dispersa entre parámetros y fidelidades que se esté suponiendo.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In \\[[1](#id17 \"Senrui Chen, Zhihan Zhang, Liang Jiang, and Steven T. Flammia. Efficient Self-Consistent Learning of Gate Set Pauli Noise. PRX Quantum, 7(1):010305, 2026. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/1pnv-t9px, doi:10.1103/1pnv-t9px.\")], in the context of unitary gate sets, this is formalized into the *Pattern Transfer Graph* (PTG): a directed graph describing all possible experiments consisting of elements of the gate set and layers of single-qubit Clifford gates (assumed to be perfect, or “free”, operations). This was generalized in \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")] to include gate sets with mid-circuit measurements. In both cases, each experiment is described by tracking a single Pauli operator through the circuit, under the assumption of a particular observable being computed at each measurement site. Here we do not directly review the graph or path constructions; however, we present the required facts for justifying them within our own notation and with resets included.\n"
msgstr "En \\[[1](#id17 \"Senrui Chen, Zhihan Zhang, Liang Jiang, and Steven T. Flammia. Efficient Self-Consistent Learning of Gate Set Pauli Noise. PRX Quantum, 7(1):010305, 2026. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/1pnv-t9px, doi:10.1103/1pnv-t9px.\")], en el contexto de conjuntos de puertas unitarias, esto se formaliza en el *Pattern Transfer Graph* (PTG): un grafo dirigido que describe todos los experimentos posibles formados por elementos del conjunto de puertas y capas de puertas Clifford de un solo qubit (que se suponen operaciones perfectas o “gratuitas”). Esto se generalizó en \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")] para incluir conjuntos de puertas con mediciones a mitad de circuito. En ambos casos, cada experimento se describe siguiendo un único operador de Pauli a través del circuito, bajo el supuesto de que se calcula un observable particular en cada punto de medición. Aquí no repasamos directamente las construcciones del grafo ni de los caminos; sin embargo, presentamos los hechos necesarios para justificarlas dentro de nuestra propia notación y con reinicios incluidos.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "While the tracking of a single Pauli operator through the circuit may be intuitive in the unitary gate set case, it does not so obviously hold in the more general Clifford-MCM-reset case, due to the non-deterministic nature of measurement. The following proposition recovers this picture even in the more general case \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")]:\n"
msgstr "Aunque seguir un único operador de Pauli a través del circuito puede ser intuitivo en el caso de conjuntos de puertas unitarias, no es tan obvio que se cumpla en el caso más general Clifford-MCM-reset, debido a la naturaleza no determinista de la medición. La siguiente proposición recupera este panorama incluso en el caso más general \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")]:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Proposition 1\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  <span id=\"proof-content\" />\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Proposición 1\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  <span id=\"proof-content\" />\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  For any $x \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M$, $y \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{M \\cup R}$, and $Q\\in \\mathcal{P}^{N \\setminus R}$, it holds that:\n"
msgstr "  Para cualquier $x \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M$, $y \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{M \\cup R}$ y $Q\\in \\mathcal{P}^{N \\setminus R}$, se cumple que:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$\n"
msgstr "$$\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\langle\\!\\langle Q \\otimes Z^y |\\otimes \\langle\\!\\langle Z^{x + y|_M} |_M \\left(\\sum_{m\\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} \\mathcal{E}_m \\otimes | m \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\right)\n"
"= \\lambda_{x,y}^Q\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(Q \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^x) |\n"
msgstr ""
"\\langle\\!\\langle Q \\otimes Z^y |\\otimes \\langle\\!\\langle Z^{x + y|_M} |_M \\left(\\sum_{m\\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} \\mathcal{E}_m \\otimes | m \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\right)\n"
"= \\lambda_{x,y}^Q\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(Q \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^x) |\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In other words, if we compute the expectation value $Z^{x + y|_M}$ on the classical register output by the MCM, then the “Heisenberg picture” evolution of an observable through the Clifford-MCM-reset gate maps $Q \\otimes Z^y \\rightarrow Q \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^x$. This fact alone enables recovery of PTG-like analysis beyond the unitary gate set case: with a particular post-processing of the measurement results, even gates with measurements and resets can be viewed as deterministically mapping one Pauli operator to another.\n"
msgstr "En otras palabras, si calculamos el valor esperado $Z^{x + y|_M}$ sobre el registro clásico producido por la MCM, entonces la evolución de un observable en la “imagen de Heisenberg” a través de la puerta Clifford-MCM-reset transforma $Q \\otimes Z^y \\rightarrow Q \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^x$. Este hecho por sí solo permite recuperar un análisis similar al del PTG más allá del caso de conjuntos de puertas unitarias: con un posprocesamiento particular de los resultados de medición, incluso las puertas con mediciones y reinicios pueden verse como una transformación determinista de un operador de Pauli en otro.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Before proving this, we write Equation [(3)](#equation-clifford-mcm-reset-form) in a more suggestive way. Note that for $x, y \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M$, it holds that $Z^{x+y} = \\sum_{m \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} (-1)^{m \\cdot (x + y)}| m \\rangle\\langle m |$, and as such (in super operator notation) we have:\n"
msgstr "Antes de demostrar esto, escribimos la Ecuación [(3)](#equation-clifford-mcm-reset-form) de una forma más sugerente. Ten en cuenta que para $x, y \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M$, se cumple que $Z^{x+y} = \\sum_{m \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} (-1)^{m \\cdot (x + y)}| m \\rangle\\langle m |$, y por tanto (en notación de superoperadores) tenemos:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{split} \\begin{aligned} \\sum_{m \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} \\mathcal{E}_m \\otimes | m \\rangle\\!\\rangle_M = \\frac{1}{2^{2|M|+|N|}}\\sum_{\\substack{x \\in\n"
"\\mathbb{Z}_2^M, y \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{M \\cup R} \\\\ Q \\in \\mathcal{P}^{N\\setminus R}}} \\lambda^Q_{x,y}  | Q \\otimes Z^y \\rangle\\!\\rangle\n"
"\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(Q \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^x) | \\otimes | Z^{x + y|_M} \\rangle\\!\\rangle_M, \\end{aligned} \\end{split}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{split} \\begin{aligned} \\sum_{m \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} \\mathcal{E}_m \\otimes | m \\rangle\\!\\rangle_M = \\frac{1}{2^{2|M|+|N|}}\\sum_{\\substack{x \\in\n"
"\\mathbb{Z}_2^M, y \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{M \\cup R} \\\\ Q \\in \\mathcal{P}^{N\\setminus R}}} \\lambda^Q_{x,y}  | Q \\otimes Z^y \\rangle\\!\\rangle\n"
"\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(Q \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^x) | \\otimes | Z^{x + y|_M} \\rangle\\!\\rangle_M, \\end{aligned} \\end{split}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "where we have applied Equation [(3)](#equation-clifford-mcm-reset-form) and collected terms, and we use the subscript $M$ to indicate the classical measurement result register, which we treat above in a similar notation to the quantum registers.\n"
msgstr "donde hemos aplicado la Ecuación [(3)](#equation-clifford-mcm-reset-form) y agrupado términos, y usamos el subíndice $M$ para indicar el registro clásico del resultado de la medición, que tratamos arriba con una notación similar a la de los registros cuánticos.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Proof. It holds that:\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Demostración. Se cumple que:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{split}\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"&\\langle\\!\\langle Q \\otimes Z^y | \\otimes \\langle\\!\\langle Z^{x + y|_M} | \\left(\\sum_{m\\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} \\mathcal{E}_m \\otimes | m \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\right)\\\\\n"
"&= \\langle\\!\\langle Q \\otimes Z^y | \\otimes \\langle\\!\\langle Z^{x + y|_M} | \\left( \\frac{1}{2^{2|M|+|N|}} \\sum_{\\substack{a \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{M \\cup R} \\\\ P \\in \\mathcal{P}^{N\\setminus R}}} \\lambda^P_{a,b}  | P \\otimes Z^b \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(P \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^a) | \\otimes | Z^{a + b|_M} \\rangle\\!\\rangle \\right) \\\\\n"
"&=\\frac{1}{2^{2|M| + |N|}}\\sum_{\\substack{a \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{M \\cup R} \\\\ P \\in \\mathcal{P}^{N \\setminus R}}} \\lambda^P_{a, b}\\underbrace{\\langle Q \\otimes Z^y, P \\otimes Z^b \\rangle}_{2^{|M| + |N|} \\delta_{P, Q}\\delta_{y,b}} \\underbrace{\\langle Z^{x + y|_M}, Z^{a + b|_M} \\rangle}_{2^{|M|}\\delta_{x+y|_M,a+b|_M}}\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(P \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^a) |\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"\\end{split}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{split}\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"&\\langle\\!\\langle Q \\otimes Z^y | \\otimes \\langle\\!\\langle Z^{x + y|_M} | \\left(\\sum_{m\\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M} \\mathcal{E}_m \\otimes | m \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\right)\\\\\n"
"&= \\langle\\!\\langle Q \\otimes Z^y | \\otimes \\langle\\!\\langle Z^{x + y|_M} | \\left( \\frac{1}{2^{2|M|+|N|}} \\sum_{\\substack{a \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{M \\cup R} \\\\ P \\in \\mathcal{P}^{N\\setminus R}}} \\lambda^P_{a,b}  | P \\otimes Z^b \\rangle\\!\\rangle\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(P \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^a) | \\otimes | Z^{a + b|_M} \\rangle\\!\\rangle \\right) \\\\\n"
"&=\\frac{1}{2^{2|M| + |N|}}\\sum_{\\substack{a \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^M, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}_2^{M \\cup R} \\\\ P \\in \\mathcal{P}^{N \\setminus R}}} \\lambda^P_{a, b}\\underbrace{\\langle Q \\otimes Z^y, P \\otimes Z^b \\rangle}_{2^{|M| + |N|} \\delta_{P, Q}\\delta_{y,b}} \\underbrace{\\langle Z^{x + y|_M}, Z^{a + b|_M} \\rangle}_{2^{|M|}\\delta_{x+y|_M,a+b|_M}}\\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(P \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^a) |\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"\\end{split}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Lastly, observe that $\\delta_{y,b}\\delta_{x+y|_M,a+b|_M} = \\delta_{y,b}\\delta_{x,a}$, and hence the final term above collapses down to the single term $\\lambda^Q_{x,y} \\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(Q \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^x) |$, yielding the desired result.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"  Por último, observa que $\\delta_{y,b}\\delta_{x+y|_M,a+b|_M} = \\delta_{y,b}\\delta_{x,a}$, y por lo tanto el último término anterior se reduce al único término $\\lambda^Q_{x,y} \\langle\\!\\langle G^\\dagger(Q \\otimes I_{N \\cap R} \\otimes Z^x) |$, lo que da el resultado deseado.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "See \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")] for development beyond this point: the definition of the PTG, paths through the PTG, and the proof that any properly-defined path corresponds to an experiment.\n"
msgstr "Consulta \\[[5](#id16 \"Zhihan Zhang, Senrui Chen, Yunchao Liu, and Liang Jiang. Generalized Cycle Benchmarking Algorithm for Characterizing Midcircuit Measurements. PRX Quantum, 6(1):010310, 2025. URL: https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310, doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.6.010310.\")] para el desarrollo posterior: la definición del PTG, los caminos a través del PTG y la demostración de que cualquier camino correctamente definido corresponde a un experimento.\n"

